// ch02 §6.4(2): "Every class type has an implicitly-defined move
// constructor with exactly one parameter, of type rvalue reference to the
// class type, irrespective of whether the C++ standard's own conditions
// for implicitly declaring one ([class.copy.ctor]) are met." It is not a
// declaration, so it is absent from the signature database -- and
// monomorphization's "a non-template constructor already accepts these
// arguments" guard consulted only that database. For the one argument
// list §6.4(2) is about, a single argument of the class's own type, the
// guard therefore reported no non-template candidate and instantiated the
// constructor *template* as well. Constructor selection does not agree:
// it picks the implicit move constructor, as §6.4(2) requires.
//
// The specialization is not harmless. A member template's body is
// type-checked once the specialization exists, so this one was checked
// with `U` = `Holder`, and `int probe = value.tag;` was rejected for
// naming a field `Holder` does not have -- in a constructor no call in
// this program ever selects. `std::expected<T, E>`, whose value
// constructor is exactly this shape, could not be constructed from
// another `std::expected<T, E>` at all.
import std;

struct Tagged {
    int tag;
};

template<typename T>
class Holder {
public:
    int origin;

    Holder() : origin{0} { return; }

    template<typename U>
    Holder(U value) : origin{1} {
        this->origin = value.tag;
        return;
    }

    virtual ~Holder() { return; }
};

int main() {
    Holder<int> a{};
    Holder<int> b{std::move(a)};
    Tagged t{3};
    Holder<int> c{std::move(t)};
    return b.origin + c.origin - 3;
}
